The classic unit mismatch: JWT's `exp` field is in **Unix seconds**, but `Date.now()` returns **Unix milliseconds** — they're off by a factor of 1000.

Depending on which direction the comparison is written, this can cause tokens to always pass or always fail. The most common pattern that silently lets expired tokens through:

```js
// BUG: Date.now() (ms, ~1.7 trillion) is always greater than exp (seconds, ~1.7 billion)
// so this condition is always true — every token looks "valid"
if (Date.now() > payload.exp) {
  return next();
}
return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
```

**Fix — pick one:**

```js
// Option 1: bring Date.now() down to seconds
if (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > payload.exp) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
}

// Option 2: bring exp up to milliseconds
if (Date.now() > payload.exp * 1000) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
}
```

Option 1 is the convention (JWT spec operates in seconds; stay in that unit).

Two other things worth checking while you're in there:

1. **`nbf` (not before)** — tokens also have a valid-from time; easy to forget.
2. **Clock skew** — if your server and token issuer clocks drift, add a small leeway (e.g. `+ 30` seconds) to avoid spurious rejections on tokens that are technically valid.

